---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`About ${SITE.author} and this blog.`}
>
  <article class="post">
    <header class="post-header">
      <p class="post-kicker">About</p>
      <h1>Hi, I'm {SITE.author}.</h1>
      <p class="post-lede">
        I'm a software engineer, and this is where I write about code, systems,
        and the craft of building things.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="post-body">
      <p>
        I'm less interested in hot takes than in ideas that survive contact with
        a real codebase. Expect notes on debugging, boring architecture, small
        tools, and the occasional postmortem.
      </p>

      <h2>What to expect</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Short, practical essays. Usually 3–6 minutes.</li>
        <li>No growth hacks, no listicles, no popups.</li>
        <li>Markdown, static HTML, and an RSS feed that actually works.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>About the site</h2>
      <p>
        A small personal blog should feel like a place to read, not a product to
        navigate. This one keeps things quiet: one column, dark by default,
        monospace details, and no comments, analytics, or cookie banner. It is
        built with <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a> and Markdown, and
        compiled to plain HTML.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="post-footnote">
      Elsewhere: <a href={SITE.github} rel="me">GitHub</a> ·
      <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
